<Case
  slug="keyward"
  title="KeyWard: self-hosted software licensing in 3 days · Thuan Nguyen"
  description="Case study: KeyWard, a self-hosted licensing service on Cloudflare Workers. License keys, seat limits, Ed25519-signed offline tokens, admin CLI and TypeScript and Rust SDKs. Built in 3 days, ~21k lines, ~200 tests."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The outcome</h2>
    <p>
      SnapPrompt needed paid licenses, and I did not want to rent a licensing platform for one app. KeyWard sells,
      issues, activates and verifies licenses for many products from one Cloudflare Worker. It took 3 days and
      it licenses SnapPrompt today.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Typo-checked license keys and per-machine activation with seat limits.</li>
      <li>Ed25519-signed license tokens. Apps and update servers verify them fully offline; private keys never leave the server.</li>
      <li>Free licenses and offline authorization for machines that never go online.</li>
      <li>Payments behind one interface, so a real gateway plugs in without touching the core.</li>
      <li>Admin API and a <code>keyward</code> command-line tool, audit log, rate limits and a transactional outbox.</li>
      <li>Client SDKs in TypeScript (browsers, Workers, Node) and Rust (Tauri and other desktop apps).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What using it looks like</h2>
    <p>Setting up a product, a plan and its signing key from the admin CLI:</p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code" aria-label="KeyWard admin CLI example"><span class="c"># create a product and a plan, then rotate its signing key</span>
keyward product create --id snapprompt --name SnapPrompt --prefix SNAP
keyward plan create --id pro --name Pro --machines 2 --price 1900 \
  --entitlements '&#123;"pro":true&#125;'
keyward key rotate</pre>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      A modular monolith with hexagonal boundaries: the licensing core knows nothing about HTTP, the database or
      payments, and the boundaries are enforced by lint rules rather than good intentions. Every write goes
      through a unit of work that records events to an outbox, so a payment, its license and its audit entry
      never disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stack: Cloudflare Workers, TypeScript, Hono, D1, Workers Rate Limiting and Wrangler, with a Rust client
      crate for desktop apps. About 21,000 lines with about 200 automated tests, written with Claude Code from a
      spec, a contracts file and a decision log, and reviewed by me.
    </p>

    <h2>What this means for your project</h2>
    <p>
      If you need an API with real money or real access behind it, I start from the contracts and the failure
      cases, not the happy path: what happens when a payment arrives twice, a machine is offline for a month, or
      a key has to be rotated.
    </p>
    <p class="note mono">The KeyWard source is private. I can walk through it on a call.</p>
  </div>
</Case>

